    "lawCode": "Add Art 9 Title 4-A §2883, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3508\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to\nsubcontracting by public authorities\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA :\nThis bill limit the ability of public authorities to contract out for\nservices that are or can be performed by employees of the public\nauthority, unless: it is more effective, it is impractical not to,\nthere is a need of specialized skills, there exists a conflict of\ninterests, it would hinder affirmative action goals, or there are\ncompliance problems with state, local, or federal laws.\n\nPublic Authorities Law entitled \"Subcontracting\" to accomplish the\nabove purpose.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nThis bill would add a new Title 4-A to the Public Authorities Law\nentitled \"Subcontracting\" to accomplish the above purpose.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nCertain public authorities have contracted out services which are\npresently performed by bargaining unit members. The purpose for such\ncontracting out has been to reduce the numbers of people on a\nutility's payroll. The contracting out in no way reduces the services\nthat the authority is providing.\n\nThere should be a review of large scale contracting out by authorities\nto see what the real impact of such contracting out would be. It\nshould not be the case that the way that authorities can become more\nefficient is to reduce the numbers of bargaining unit employees.\nThere are a variety of ways that the authority can work safer and more\nefficiently in order to reduce costs. Simply requiring authorities to\ndo this by reducing their payroll is poor public policy and should be\nstopped.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nA.10924 (2003-04)\nA.2106 (2005-06)\nA.1371 (2007-08)\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATION FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nApril first next succeeding enactment.\n",
